==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|fro|estanson}}, {{m|fro|estanchon}}, (Modern {{cog|fr|étançon}}), from {{m|fro|estance||a stay, a prop}}, from {{der|en|la|stāns||standing}}, present participle of {{m|la|stō}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|stănʹshən|stänʹshən}}, {{IPA|en|/ˈstanʃən/|/ˈstɑːnʃən/|a=UK}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-stanchion.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{enPR|stănʹshən}}, {{IPA|en|/ˈstænʃən/|a=US}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}
[[File:Stanchions and velvet rope - 37th G8 summit in Deauville 002.jpg|thumb|Stanchions supporting velvet rope]]

# A [[vertical]] [[pole]], [[post]], or [[support]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1938|author=w:Xavier Herbert|title=[[w:Capricornia (novel)|Capricornia]]|location=New York|publisher=D. Appleton-Century|year_published=1943|chapter=IX|page=149|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/capricornianovel00herb/page/149/mode/1up|passage=The train began to move. Lace walked with it, holding a '''stanchion'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Jay McInerney|title={{w|Bright Lights, Big City (novel)|Bright Lights, Big City}}|year=1984|page=10|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/brightlightsbigc0000mcin/page/10/mode/1up|isbn=0394726413|passage=You cross under the rusting '''stanchions''' of the old elevated highway and walk out to the pier.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2013|author=w:J. M. Coetzee|title=w:The Childhood of Jesus|location=Melbourne, Australia|publisher=The Text Publishing Company|chapter=27|page=268|passage=He staggers against a '''stanchion''', trips over a rope, and tumbles into the space between the quay and the steel plates of the freighter.}}
# A [[framework]] of such posts, used to [[secure]] or [[confine]] [[cattle]].

